00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.040
So there's a number
of advantages

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.740
of using multi-tenant clusters.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.960
To understand the
benefits, let's actually

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.210
step back and think
about what happens

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:47.420
when you have separate
cluster for each tenant.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.360
First, for each
additional cluster,

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.980
you'll have additional
control plane

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.050
overhead and management burden.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:57.110
Second, in a large
system where perhaps you

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.780
have hundreds or even
thousands of namespaces,

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.620
or hundreds or thousands
of different tenants,

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.450
you need tools to manage
those many clusters at scale.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:10.880
Even with very good
tooling, it can

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.230
be hard to manage
those many clusters.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:20.630
More importantly, when the
free or idle resources of each

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:25.370
cluster cannot be reused by
other tenants' applications.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.950
On the contrary, if you
use multi-tenant clusters,

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:30.890
then obviously you have
lower control plane overhead

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:34.100
and better resource
utilization, and also you'll

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.970
have less resource
fragmentation.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.610
To illustrate with
an example, let's say

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:43.370
you have one team
which is focusing

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.100
on real-time applications,
which are presumably

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.520
more active during the day,
and you have another team that

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.520
is focusing on batch
applications which

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.460
can be done during the night.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.570
Sharing the resources
for the two teams

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.500
can provide significant savings.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.350
Kubernetes can
optimize resource usage

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.050
by efficiently
bin-packing applications

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.250
from different tenants.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:11.200
Also, it's much faster
to provision resources

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.650
for a tenant within
an existing cluster

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.040
than to bootstrap a
cluster from the scratch.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.970
Last but not least, all the
tenants of a multi-tenant GKE

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:28.870
cluster can share any
sort of extensions, CRDs,

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:30.370
or controllers.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.650
And also, because they're
sharing the same control

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:37.780
plane, any sort of
configuration settings,

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.750
reliability measures,
security measures,

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.670
are automatically scheduled
by all the tenants.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.880
So if there is a lot of
benefits, what's the problem?

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.450
Fine-grained visibility
into resource usage

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:53.600
becomes very important in
multi-tenant environments

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.930
because in the absence
of fine-grained metrics,

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:01.160
it can be hard to
estimate which tenant

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.370
is consuming what portion
of the cluster's resources.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:10.700
It can be tough to determine
which tenant has perhaps

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:15.050
introduced a bug that led to a
sudden spike in resource usage.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.680
Or it can be hard to identify
the prodigal tenant who

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.970
perhaps themselves
don't know that they

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.000
are wasting resources.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.010
Let's actually look
a little bit deeper

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:29.810
to understand who exactly
needs fine-grained visibility

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:30.770
and why they need it.

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:37.420
If you look at finance and
procurement professionals,

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.710
they need to understand
actual resource usage numbers

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:46.830
because they need to have a
concrete basis for estimating

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.340
charge-backs.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.470
If you don't have the
real usage numbers

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:54.550
and you resort to ballpark
estimates or rough heuristics,

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.830
it can, again, be unfair
to some of the tenants.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.430
Also, as a finance
officer, you want

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:03.010
to make sure that the
cost versus Benefit

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:05.230
is justified for
different initiatives

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.023
within your organization.

00:06:07.023 --> 00:06:08.440
You want to make
sure that you are

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:12.160
able to provide good forecasts
for planning and budgeting

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.980
purposes, all of
which, again, require

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:16.930
a good understanding
of usage patterns

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:19.550
and the historical trends.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:23.190
If you are a site reliability
engineer, SRE, or a cluster

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:26.870
administrator, it
is very important

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:31.040
to catch any sort of
configuration errors or bugs

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:34.070
that can lead to
spikes in usages.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:37.010
A simplistic example could
be that one of your teams

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:41.360
has accidentally forgotten to
wind down after running a test.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.360
Or after running updates
for server applications,

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.110
you might realize that suddenly
the inter-region network

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:50.960
traffic, which is the
expensive kind of traffic,

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.290
has suddenly become 20
times the original amount.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:55.910
In those scenarios
you want to be

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:59.990
able to drill down and catch
the source of the problem

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:02.770
as quickly as possible.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:06.580
On similar lines, if you are
an IT director or a product

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:10.420
manager, you want to make sure
that the fundamental business

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:15.790
aspects of your product or
application are making sense.

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.910
For example, is the revenue
generated by your product

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:26.080
more than the cost of building
the product and operating it?

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.580
Are your team's expenses
within the allocated budget?

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.680
Are you well-planned
for the future?

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:35.110
For example, if you
anticipate that your users

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.550
for your product are
going to become 20 times

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:41.890
within the next year,
you want to have

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:44.050
a good sense of
how many resources

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:47.590
you have to reserve to
sustain that growth.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:51.130
And overall, in
most of the cases,

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:56.550
providing a dashboard or
report to each engineering team

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.830
where the teams can look
for themselves the usage

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:02.650
trends for their
applications, that in itself

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.040
can provide significant savings
because ultimately, nobody

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:09.190
wants to waste resources.

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:13.210
So there's several use cases
of fine-grained metrics,

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:16.270
and GKE Usage Metering
feature is the solution

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:18.370
to those use cases.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:24.550
The feature allows you to
see your project's resource

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:28.990
usage broken down by Kubernetes
namespaces and labels,

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:33.990
and attributed into
meaningful entities.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:37.860
The fine-grain metrics are
captured at quad level.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:42.429
The user can then filter
group and aggregate

00:08:42.429 --> 00:08:45.480
the fine-grain metrics
to calculate usage

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.580
for different entities.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:51.420
The entity can be a department,
an environment, a team,

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:55.880
an application, whatever
it makes sense to you.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:58.880
Looking into the key
aspects of the feature,

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.630
we are currently at Beta.

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:06.890
Usage can be segregated based
on both Kubernetes namespaces

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:10.730
and labels, and the combination
gives a lot of flexibility

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:12.440
to the users.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:14.540
Whether you want to
look at a micro level

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:18.110
and understand
which sub-component

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:21.410
of your application is taking
up most of your resources,

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:23.210
or you want to look
at the macro level

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:27.430
and understand the usage for
the whole department or team,

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:31.910
or you just want to estimate
the cost of running a load test,

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.240
you can arrive at all those
numbers using the feature

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:39.920
provided you use labels
and namespaces to organize

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:41.420
your workloads.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.410
And the last part
is really important.

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:46.600
For the benefit of
those of you who

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:49.930
are not familiar with Kubernetes
namespaces and labels,

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:53.680
let me take a quick detour.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.930
Namespaces provide a mechanism
to split Kubernetes objects

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:02.440
into separate,
non-overlapping groups.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:05.020
Labels, on the other
hand, are arbitrary

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:07.450
key value pairs that
you can attach to any

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.880
of your Kubernetes objects.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.560
To illustrate with
an example, let's say

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:15.490
if you have a company which
has 10 different departments,

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:18.220
and let's say each
department on average

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.160
has 15 different
microservices, then

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:23.470
one way to organize
your workloads

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:27.910
would be to have 10 namespaces,
one for each department.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:29.710
And for each of
your microservice,

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:34.420
you can attach identifying
attributes such as application

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:36.800
name, sub-component name.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.340
You can attach the tier name,
tier being the front end

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:41.980
or back end or whatever
makes sense for you.

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:46.270
Or you can even attach the
release or version IDs.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.700
The key is to make
sure you organize

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:51.640
your workloads in a way
that is meaningful to you.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:55.120
If everything gets deployed
into the default namespace

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:57.190
and you don't use any
labels, then you'll

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:59.890
not be able to take advantage
of the full potential

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:02.200
of the feature.

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:05.250
So back to the feature.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:07.200
In terms of resource
types, we are currently

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:12.360
supporting CPU, memory, PD,
GPUs, and network egress.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:17.850
At GE, we'll provide support
for even TPU and custom machine

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:19.470
types.

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:22.620
In terms of reported
metrics, we are currently

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:25.170
supporting
request-based metrics.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:28.200
Yang will talk more about the
metrics a little bit later,

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:33.210
but at GE we will support even
consumption-based metrics.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:34.950
With that, I'll
turn it over to Yang

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:36.560
for a high-level
architectural view.

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:37.060
Yang?

00:11:42.813 --> 00:11:43.980
YANG GUAN: Thank you, Madhu.

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:51.240
I'd like to start with talking
about how resources usage is

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.220
measured in GKE.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:57.990
In this picture, the gray box
represents the GKE master VM,

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:01.110
which is the control plan
of a Kubernetes cluster,

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:04.950
and the yellow boxes represent
nodes in your cluster.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:08.040
To measure resource usage
in your GKE cluster,

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.550
we have developed this
usage metering agent, which

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:15.450
also runs on the GKE master
VM, so it's totally managed

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:17.400
by Google and you
don't need to worry

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:21.980
about deploying it and
keeping it up and running.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:26.780
To measure a pod's
CPU and memory usage,

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:30.470
the metering agent talks
to the Kube API server

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:33.530
and collects information such
as the starting and ending

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:37.340
time of a pod, how
many CPUs and memories

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:41.240
are being requested by the pod,
as well as the pod's namespace

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.320
and the labels.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:46.120
In a very similar
fashion, resource usage

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:49.630
of storage or even
GPUs can be recorded.

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:52.720
But not all information is
available from the Kube API

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:56.770
server, and one of the missing
pieces is network statistic.

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:59.690
You might be wondering,
why do I need to know that?

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:02.450
Well, the natural
statistics gives you

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:05.630
insight into the behavior
of your application.

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:08.260
For example, if you
see a sudden spike

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.340
in traffic sent into
[INAUDIBLE] region,

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:15.270
that might indicate there
is a bug, or even worse,

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.290
your application
is under attack.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:20.520
What is more, sending
traffic to a certain region

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:24.220
it is not for free, so you want
to collect network statistics

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.650
so that when you
perform cost allocation,

00:13:26.650 --> 00:13:30.070
you can allocate the
network egress to tenants

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:32.050
that generate the traffic.

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.720
To solve this problem,
we also developed

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.940
this natural
monitoring component

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:40.660
that is deployed into our
GKE cluster as a daemon set.

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:42.400
So there is one
component running

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.920
on each node in your cluster.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:47.590
The component talks
to the system kernel

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:51.520
to grab raw network statistics,
and sends the statistic

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:53.860
to the centralized
metering agent.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:57.130
The metering agent
processes the statistic

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:59.860
and can convert
it to usage record

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:03.070
and send the record
BigQuery queried together

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:04.690
with the records
for other resources,

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:07.570
such as the CPU and the memory.

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:10.840
Once the usage records
are available in BigQuery,

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:13.420
you can now perform
various analyses,

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:17.110
such as gauging the aggregate
user CPU usage for a given

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:21.070
namespace, or calculate the
total memory bytes being

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.960
requested by their application.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:25.780
But a more interesting
use case would

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:31.990
be to use the usage data and the
joint with the GCP billing data

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:33.790
to do cost allocation.

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:36.460
On one hand, from
GKE usage data,

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:39.190
you know how much a
resource is being used

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:41.630
by a team on an application.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:44.590
And on the other
hand, GCP billing data

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:46.420
tells you how much
you are paying

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:50.170
for a piece of cloud
resource in GCP.

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:52.780
Joining them together
allows you to understand

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:54.700
how much a team
on the application

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:58.630
is actually costing you, even
though it's running on a shield

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:02.790
computer infrastructure.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:05.260
And once you perform
the cost break down,

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:07.770
you can even export
data in BigQuery

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:12.450
to external tools, such as
Data Studio, for visualization.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.450
And we actually provide you
with a Data Studio template

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.180
to make the process
easier for you.

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.380
Before we move on,
I'd like to talk

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:26.580
briefly about why we choose
to collect resource requests.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:28.980
As you might have
already known, there

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:33.540
are three metrics related in
the Kubernetes resource model.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:38.310
The resources request
represents the amount

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:41.550
of resource being requested
or reserved for a pod,

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:45.000
and the consumption
represents the actual amount

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:48.090
of resource being used
as a runtime by a pod.

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:52.530
And lastly, resource limit poses
the upper bound on the resource

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:53.700
consumption.

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:56.340
Among these three
metrics, we choose

00:15:56.340 --> 00:16:01.260
to measure resource request
and use it for cost allocation

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.740
because we believe it's
more relevant to the cost

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:07.620
of your cluster, and
let me explain why.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:11.790
So first of all, when
kube-scheduler schedules pods,

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:15.630
it uses the pod request
to see whether the pod can

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:17.200
be a fit on a node.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:20.070
And once the pod is
assigned to a node,

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:22.740
the scheduler
considers the amount

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:24.870
of the requested
resource being reserved

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:28.020
for that particular pod,
regardless whether a party is

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:30.480
actually using it.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:33.510
Kube-scheduler will stop
assigning more pods to a node

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:39.270
if they node does not have
enough unreserved capacity.

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.100
And if none of the
nodes in your cluster

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:46.440
has enough unreserved capacity,
unassigned pods in your system

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:50.950
become pending, and that is when
the cluster auto-scaler starts

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:56.680
to resize your cluster by adding
more nodes into your cluster.

00:16:56.680 --> 00:17:00.240
And this is when the cost
of your cluster goes up.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:03.090
So based on these
reasons, we believe

00:17:03.090 --> 00:17:07.560
resource request is more
relevant to the cost

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:10.560
of your cluster, and
decided to use request

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:16.680
to allocate the overall
cost of your cluster.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:19.829
And with that, I would like
to pass the floor to David

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:22.589
to talk about how GKE
usage and metering could

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:23.909
be used in Spotify.

00:17:26.475 --> 00:17:27.642
DAVID XIA: I just need that.

00:17:31.044 --> 00:17:32.320
Hey, everyone.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:36.130
Thinks Thanks, Yang, for
that great explanation.

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:36.850
My name is David.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:41.440
I work at Spotify as an
infrastructure engineer.

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:44.500
My team specifically works on
migrating Spotify stateless

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:50.150
back end services from
GCE right now to GKE.

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:52.360
And I'm actually glad to
be here today to show you

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:56.950
how this new GKE feature that
they've built looks and feels.

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:03.050
First, a little
bit about Spotify

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:06.660
and the scale of our GCP usage.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:10.700
So we have over
1,000 developers.

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:13.410
We have created-- that
number is correct--

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:17.000
2,800 GCP projects
all under one org.

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.850
That's a lot of projects.

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:21.710
And spread out across
all of those projects

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:25.130
are tens of thousands
of GCE instances.

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:31.150
So that's kind of the scale
that we're running at.

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:32.770
And how do we use GCP?

00:18:32.770 --> 00:18:35.020
How do we structure our usage?

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:39.230
Teams will own at
least one project.

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:44.380
Usually we tell people to create
one project per logical system.

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:47.140
And teams own
their GCE instances

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:48.130
within their projects.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:51.250
They're responsible for
provisioning that capacity,

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:54.340
for deploying services onto
them, and monitoring them,

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:56.360
so they truly own
it in every sense.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.680
The centralized infrastructure
teams don't really do that.

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:01.810
We provide tools for them.

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:06.430
And you get cost breakdown
for free in your GCP billing

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:12.050
invoice by project, so
that's just easy there.

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:16.430
The lack of multi-tenancy,
though, adds operational burden

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:17.160
on teams.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:21.410
So what I just described
in the first three bullets

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:24.650
is how the vast majority of
Spotify's back-end services

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:28.460
are structured on GCP, and
as you can probably tell

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:31.130
it's a single tenant design.

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:34.280
So people just run their
service on one machine,

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:36.080
and if they need
multiple replicas,

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:38.360
they just get more instances.

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:41.713
So it's very, very poor
resource utilization and it

00:19:41.713 --> 00:19:43.380
puts a lot of operational
burden on them

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:45.632
because they need to know
how many instances they

00:19:45.632 --> 00:19:47.090
need, what machine
types they need,

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:49.190
and manage all that themselves.

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:53.060
And we are trying to migrate
to GKE because it reduces

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:55.520
this operational burden
by abstracting away

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:56.570
these instances.

00:19:56.570 --> 00:20:02.480
And also, it makes it easier for
us on the infrastructure side

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:06.812
to increase that resource
consumption efficiency.

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:13.210
So how do we plan on using GKE?

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.680
We have many production
GKE clusters.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:20.450
We put them all in one project.

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:25.250
The team that I work on owns
these multi-tenant clusters.

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:29.140
And similar to GCP
projects, we tell

00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:33.010
people to create one Kubernetes
namespace per logical system,

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:34.840
not per team.

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:37.690
It makes it easier
because people inevitably

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:39.750
transfer systems from
one team to another,

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.700
they maybe split something
out, and it's just easier

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:45.950
if it's already in
its own namespace.

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:48.160
And if we want to
label things, let's say

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:50.020
we have common services
across namespaces,

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:51.687
we'll just apply the
same label there so

00:20:51.687 --> 00:20:54.460
that you can later query
for the same workload

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:56.170
across multiple namespaces.

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:58.300
So we use labels in that way.

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:00.970
And same way with projects.

00:21:00.970 --> 00:21:04.300
A team can own many namespaces.

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:05.990
And how do we do the
ownership mapping?

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:08.240
How do we tell which namespace
is owned by which team?

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:12.230
We use-- we require,
actually, an annotation.

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:15.100
So when a team creates
the namespace YAML,

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:18.370
we require they have a
specific annotation that

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:20.278
is called the owner annotation.

00:21:20.278 --> 00:21:21.820
And then we do
validation and linting

00:21:21.820 --> 00:21:23.800
before we allow
them to merge it in.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:28.395
And that value must be an
engineering team's email,

00:21:28.395 --> 00:21:29.770
and we do some
validation to make

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:34.270
sure it's actually a real
team that's enabled to use GKE

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:38.440
and there's no typos
or anything like that.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:43.120
So, namespace
ownership can change.

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:44.890
It's done very easily
just by changing

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:47.050
the value of that annotation.

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:51.250
We also require loose resource
quotas for all namespaces.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:52.420
This is done very easily.

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:56.610
You can just enable it in
your mission controller.

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:59.250
And right now we're
just only migrating

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:01.387
stateless back-end
services for now.

00:22:01.387 --> 00:22:02.970
We're not doing
anything [INAUDIBLE],,

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:05.680
so when I show you
the demo later,

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:07.860
that's why you'll only
see CPU and memory.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:09.900
You won't see anything
like persistent disks.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:16.280
And just a little bit
about our migration

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:20.570
challenges, and this is where
the feature comes into play.

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:23.780
So when you're using GCP,
you get billing reports that

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:25.460
are broken down by project.

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:26.780
Very simple.

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:31.460
And our finance team uses
those invoices to kind of get

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:34.102
insight into which team
is using resources,

00:22:34.102 --> 00:22:35.060
what kind of resources.

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:38.750
They use it to project
how our costs are

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:40.950
going to grow over time.

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:42.530
So what's happening now?

00:22:42.530 --> 00:22:46.370
You can probably already tell
that when we migrate to GKE

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:48.800
and all of these clusters
are multi-tenant,

00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:53.140
they're used by many teams,
and they're all in one project,

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:56.320
the billing reports are going to
look a little bit funky, right?

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:00.430
What's going to happen
is my team's project

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:04.360
costs are going to go
up as people migrate,

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:06.010
and those other
teams' projects' costs

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:07.597
are going to go
down because they're

00:23:07.597 --> 00:23:08.680
moving their compute away.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:10.540
They're kind of
getting rid of it.

00:23:10.540 --> 00:23:16.270
And it looks to finance like,
why is this team using so much

00:23:16.270 --> 00:23:16.810
compute?

00:23:16.810 --> 00:23:18.200
What was going on?

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:21.100
Are they just being wasteful?

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:23.590
Are they running
something insane?

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:25.870
But it's actually
kind of hidden.

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:30.900
What's happening is that we're
subsuming other teams' costs.

00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:37.480
So migrating to GKE without
any fine-grained cost breakdown

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.480
means we can't tell how
many resources each team

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:43.090
is consuming, which system is
suddenly using more resources.

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:44.730
Maybe it's due to a bug.

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:48.390
Which systems are being wasteful
in their resource requests?

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:51.690
But luckily, GKE Usage Metering
helps us solve these problems.

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:59.360
And how do we plan on
using GKE Usage Metering?

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:01.880
So like I said, our finance
team will gain insight

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:03.370
into the cost breakdown.

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:05.540
They can forecast future costs.

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:10.430
They can analyze the seasonality
of different systems.

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:13.550
Individual teams can
also see their own costs.

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:17.460
We want them to see their own
costs so that they're aware.

00:24:17.460 --> 00:24:22.590
And my team, or other central
teams that provide tools,

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:24.900
can identify candidate
systems for cost reduction.

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:29.850
So say we noticed your
request saying eight CPUs

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:32.700
for each of your pods,
but they only really need

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:35.170
to use one even at peak load.

00:24:35.170 --> 00:24:38.730
So we can investigate
these pod resource requests

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:42.420
for anomalies or
unreasonable values.

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:47.760
We can even try to look
at, maybe this service that

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:50.900
doesn't need to be running all
the time or is not critical,

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:53.220
we can schedule it onto
some preemptable nodes

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:55.900
and save some money there.

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:59.990
So I think that
brings me to the demo.

