00:32:41.580 --> 00:32:42.420
that you can select.

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:47.020
That's it for the demo.

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:48.385
Let me go back to the slides.

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:53.610
Thank you.

00:32:53.610 --> 00:32:54.110
All right.

00:32:54.110 --> 00:32:59.780
So how does this experience
compare with and without Usage

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:00.830
Metering?

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:03.330
What are you actually getting
by using this feature?

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:05.747
You don't need to do all the
manual collection of the data

00:33:05.747 --> 00:33:07.220
yourself.

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:08.340
They did some great magic.

00:33:08.340 --> 00:33:11.420
I don't know how it works, but
it does, and I trust it to work

00:33:11.420 --> 00:33:14.030
and that saves me a lot of time.

00:33:14.030 --> 00:33:16.790
The BigQuery sink makes
it super easy to run

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:19.790
ad hoc queries in BigQuery.

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:20.780
You can just write SQL.

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:22.740
You can run some ad hoc queries.

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:26.420
You can schedule jobs if
you want them to recur.

00:33:26.420 --> 00:33:28.490
The docs are great.

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:32.280
You can just walk through them
in a very short amount of time,

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:35.450
and it's pretty
self-explanatory.

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:38.270
And the templated Data Studio
visualizations are great.

00:33:38.270 --> 00:33:41.420
You can also plug in your own
visualization if you want,

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:44.300
or maybe you have another
tool that you prefer.

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:46.160
Anything that
connects to BigQuery.

00:33:46.160 --> 00:33:48.110
You're welcome to use that.

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:51.785
And the GKE Usage
Metering can be

00:33:51.785 --> 00:33:53.660
used to check other
metering tools' accuracy.

00:33:53.660 --> 00:33:56.720
Maybe you have another tool
that also collects data and does

00:33:56.720 --> 00:33:59.390
the same thing, but you
want to check that accuracy.

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:01.560
Maybe you want to
check each other.

00:34:01.560 --> 00:34:05.220
That's another
possible use case.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:05.720
Cool.

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:08.262
So with that I'm going to give
it back, throw it back to Yang

00:34:08.262 --> 00:34:10.469
to talk about best practices.

00:34:10.469 --> 00:34:10.969
Thanks.

00:34:15.072 --> 00:34:16.239
YANG GUAN: Thank you, David.

00:34:16.239 --> 00:34:18.760
This is an excellent demo.

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:22.719
So to quickly follow
up, as demonstrated,

00:34:22.719 --> 00:34:27.280
GKE Usage Metering provides you
with fine-grained visibility

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:29.989
into the resource
usage in your cluster.

00:34:29.989 --> 00:34:31.630
So I want to quickly
talk about how

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:36.500
you can gather the most out of
GKE usage metering in practice.

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:40.100
As shown, we allow
you to group usage

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:43.400
by Kubernetes
namespaces and labels.

00:34:43.400 --> 00:34:48.050
So it's ideal for you to
standardize and enforce

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:50.929
a namespace and
labeling strategy

00:34:50.929 --> 00:34:53.420
within your organization.

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:55.760
First of all, it's highly
recommended for you

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:59.090
to use namespace as the
boundaries for either systems

00:34:59.090 --> 00:35:00.050
or teams.

00:35:00.050 --> 00:35:01.970
In particular, do
not put everything

00:35:01.970 --> 00:35:03.750
into the default namespace.

00:35:03.750 --> 00:35:06.780
This not only helps you
to understand its usage,

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:10.880
but it also makes it easier
for you to manage code

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:14.210
and the policy in your cluster.

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:17.000
Secondly, it is
recommended for you

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:20.270
to define a set of
[INAUDIBLE] required labels

00:35:20.270 --> 00:35:23.810
so that you can associate
your essential attributes

00:35:23.810 --> 00:35:29.010
with Kubernetes objects, such as
application IDs or team names.

00:35:29.010 --> 00:35:33.100
Overall, the namespace and
labeling strategy you choose

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:36.860
should reflect your
organizational structure

00:35:36.860 --> 00:35:41.430
so that you can easily map
resource usage to, for example,

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:46.030
a team within your
company or business unit.

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:48.510
So we have talked
about how GKE usage

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:50.430
metering can do right now.

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:51.780
What is next?

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:55.080
We are looking forward to
launching the feature into beta

00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:58.680
in this quarter,
and in beta we will

00:35:58.680 --> 00:36:02.730
be supporting consumption-based
metrics together

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:05.830
with the existing
request-based metrics.

00:36:05.830 --> 00:36:09.000
So this will allow
you to identify areas

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:12.050
where resources are
either over-requested or

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:13.500
under-requested.

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:16.830
We hope it can help you to
better optimize the resource

00:36:16.830 --> 00:36:19.530
usage of your GKE cluster.

00:36:19.530 --> 00:36:23.400
We will also be supporting
more computer resources, namely

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:27.840
the TensorFlow processing
units, as well as GCE instance

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:29.220
with customer machine types.

00:36:32.120 --> 00:36:34.660
So this brings me to the
end of the presentation.

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:38.860
To quickly wrap up, we talked
about the numerous advantages

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:42.690
to operate a
multi-tenant GKE cluster.

00:36:42.690 --> 00:36:46.270
Namely, it makes the
cluster management easier

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:50.470
and also improves the
resource utilization.

00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:54.220
We understand in a
multi-tenant GKE cluster,

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:56.770
getting insight
into resource usage,

00:36:56.770 --> 00:37:00.120
particular per tenant
resource usage is important.

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:03.790
And the GKE Usage Metering is
designed to solve this problem.

00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:08.290
It generates a usage record and
exports it to BigQuery together

00:37:08.290 --> 00:37:11.770
with the associated
namespace and labels,

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:16.600
which allows you to flexibly
grouping and filtering usage.

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:20.980
And also, you can export
the data from BigQuery

00:37:20.980 --> 00:37:23.480
to Data Studio
for visualization,

00:37:23.480 --> 00:37:26.680
and we provide a default
Data Studio template to make

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:29.440
the process easier for you.

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:32.590
If you want to know more
about GKE Usage Metering,

00:37:32.590 --> 00:37:37.150
we have links to our blog
post and the documentation.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:42.290
If you want to let us know the
feedback about this feature,

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:44.740
we also have a link to
the feedback form here.

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:47.740
With that, thank you
very much for your time.

00:37:47.740 --> 00:37:51.390
